SECTIONS.insert(1, {
    "id": "dependencies-and-pipelines",
    "kicker": "Dependencies",
    "title": "When Can Two Pieces Of Work Run Together?",
    "summary": "Prerequisite: the opening lesson on elapsed time and completion rate. Here we distinguish waiting for a result from waiting for a resource.",
    "body": [
        "Suppose a program reads an image, resizes it, and classifies it. Classification needs the resized image. Resizing needs the original image. These requirements impose an order even if three processors are available. A dependency means that one operation needs something another operation has yet to produce.",
        "Now introduce a second image. Its read does not need the first image's classification. A reader can fetch the second image while another device resizes the first. Different images can therefore occupy different stages at once. This arrangement is a pipeline. The order within each image remains intact while work on different images overlaps.",
        "Use an invented teaching example: reading takes 2 milliseconds, resizing takes 5, and classification takes 3. Give each stage its own resource, allow only one image per stage at a time, and assume transfers between stages cost nothing. Images are independent and arrive ready to be processed. The first image finishes after 2 + 5 + 3 = 10 milliseconds.",
        "The next image can read from time 2 to time 4, but resizing remains occupied until time 7. It must wait. Resizing then runs from 7 to 12 and classification from 12 to 15. A third image can finish at time 20. Once work is flowing, the slowest stage allows one completion every 5 milliseconds, or 200 images per second. That rate describes a continuing stream; a single image still has to pass through all three stages.",
        "For n ready images, this model gives a total time of 10 + (n − 1) × 5 milliseconds: the first image takes 10 ms to fill the stages, and each later image finishes 5 ms after the one before it. The last image still has to leave the pipeline; its remaining work is included in that formula. For ten images, the total is 55 ms, compared with 100 ms if each image is finished before the next begins. The pipeline overlaps work; it does not remove any reading, resizing, or classification.",
        "There is also a storage obligation. The reader can produce one image every 2 milliseconds, or 0.5 images per millisecond; resizing can consume one every 5 milliseconds, or 0.2 per millisecond. Once the stages are flowing, the waiting queue grows by about 0.3 image per millisecond if both keep running. This rate calculation is an invented model, not a measurement. A buffer stores waiting work and absorbs a temporary mismatch, but it cannot make the slower stage faster. If the reader can be paused, it must wait when the buffer is full. If new images cannot be paused, excess images must be dropped or stored somewhere else; otherwise the finite buffer eventually overflows.",
        "The separate-resource assumption matters just as much as the dependency order. If resizing and classification occupy the same processor and cannot overlap, that processor must provide 8 milliseconds of work per image. Its capacity is at most 125 images per second. Drawing three boxes does not create three independent resources. Shared memory connections can introduce a similar limit even when processors are separate.",
        "Check a complete schedule for that shared processor. No image is ready until the first read finishes at time 2. Resize the first from 2 to 7 and classify it from 7 to 10. The reader can prepare the second image while this happens; resize it from 10 to 15 and classify it from 15 to 18. Continuing this order finishes ten images at time 2 + 10 × 8 = 82 milliseconds. Nothing can beat 82 under these assumptions: all 80 milliseconds of processor work must happen after time 2. The schedule attains the bound because reading later images keeps up and waiting images have storage. Merely calculating 80 milliseconds of work would omit startup.",
        "Concurrency means several activities can be in progress over the same period. Parallel execution means work actually occurs simultaneously. A program can make progress on another activity while one waits for input, even on a single processor. The linked OSTEP chapters explain these reasons for using multiple threads and how a producer and consumer share a bounded buffer. Our pipeline calculations are separate teaching examples with explicitly idealized timing.",
        "Two conference papers connect this distinction to model training. WLB-LLM (OSDI 2025) studies documents of different lengths. A batch is a group of inputs processed together. If workers receive equal numbers of documents but some documents are much longer, equal counts can leave one worker with more work. WLB-LLM packs variable-length documents across pipeline batches and splits work by document across workers, aiming to reduce the time spent waiting for the worker that finishes last.",
        "Its tested framework reports 1.23× over Plain-4D and 1.19× over Fixed-4D. Other comparisons report 7.5% and 3.4% improvements over static per-sequence and per-document sharding. Sharding means dividing work or data into portions assigned to different workers; static assignments stay fixed for the comparison. These names identify the paper's comparison systems and policies. The reported gains belong to those configurations and do not determine the gain for another workload.",
        "FSMoE (ASPLOS 2025) studies mixture-of-experts models, which send each token, a unit of input, to selected specialist parts of the model. Its approach measures time spent routing inputs, moving data, running the specialists, and exchanging update values. It then schedules independent communication and calculation together. It also adjusts work division between the forward pass (computing predictions) and backward pass (calculating model updates). The opportunity comes from work whose inputs are already available while another operation waits.",
        "The authors evaluated two groups of graphics processing units, or GPUs, connected for training. They report 1.18×–1.22× over their Tutel/PipeMoE baselines in 1,458 configured mixture-of-experts layer cases, and 1.19×–3.01× over DeepSpeed-MoE/Tutel in tested GPT-2 and Mixtral models. A layer is one stage of a model's calculation. A result for an individual layer and a result for training a model cover different amounts of work.",
        "These reported results come from different experiments and baselines, are not directly comparable, and have not been reproduced by this course. WLB-LLM illustrates reducing uneven work across workers; FSMoE illustrates scheduling independent work together. In either case, trace the dependencies and shared resources before expecting a shorter completion time.",
    ],
    "applications": [
        "Training: exchange a completed portion of an update while calculating another portion whose inputs are already available.",
        "Serving: prepare another request while an earlier request executes, provided memory and execution resources permit it.",
        "Chip design: supply storage between stages and check whether shared connections prevent the intended schedule.",
    ],
    "exercise": {
        "question": "With separate resources, reduce resizing from 5 to 2 milliseconds. Reading remains 2 and classification remains 3. How long do the first image and ten images take? Which stage now limits the continuing completion rate? Then put resizing and classification on one shared processor. How does the ten-image time change?",
        "answer": "The first image takes 2 + 2 + 3 = 7 milliseconds. Classification is now the slowest stage, allowing a completion every 3 milliseconds. Ten images finish after 7 + 9 × 3 = 34 milliseconds. The long-stream rate approaches 1,000 ÷ 3, about 333 images per second. With one processor for resizing and classification, each image needs 2 + 3 = 5 milliseconds on that processor. Start at time 2 and finish each image before starting the next: ten images take 2 + 10 × 5 = 52 milliseconds. The first still finishes at time 7, but subsequent completions are five milliseconds apart. These schedules assume independent images, adequate buffering, a separate reader, and no transfer overhead.",
    },
    "sources": [
        ("https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-intro.pdf", "OSTEP, chapter 26, section 26.1: parallel work and progress during I/O waits"),
        ("https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-cv.pdf", "OSTEP, chapter 30, section 30.2: producer/consumer work with a bounded buffer"),
        ("https://www.usenix.org/system/files/osdi25-wang-zheng.pdf", "WLB-LLM (OSDI 2025), sections 3–7: uneven work, variable-length packing, sharding, and evaluation"),
        ("https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.10714", "FSMoE (ASPLOS 2025) author manuscript, sections 3–6: profiled scheduling, communication overlap, and evaluated configurations"),
        ("analysis/osdi-asplos-2025-first-principles-service-traces.md", "OSDI–ASPLOS service traces: dependencies in serving and training; illustrative examples"),
    ],
})

SECTIONS.insert(next(i for i, section in enumerate(SECTIONS) if section["id"] == "s7"), {
    "id": "queues-and-batching",
    "kicker": "Sharing",
    "title": "Doing More Work Can Make Someone Wait Longer",
    "summary": "Prerequisites: elapsed time and dependencies. Separate waiting, useful execution, and the policy that decides whose turn comes next.",
    "body": [
        "A request arrives while a machine is busy. Its calculation may need only a few milliseconds, but it cannot start yet. A queue is the set of work waiting for access. The order in which that work receives access is a scheduling policy. A faster calculation and a better policy solve different problems: one reduces how long the resource is occupied; the other changes who waits. All numerical examples below are invented for teaching.",
        "Suppose a single worker takes 10 milliseconds per request, never overlaps requests, and initially has no work. Five requests arrive together at time zero. If it handles them in order, they finish at 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50 milliseconds. The average completion delay is 30 milliseconds even though every calculation takes only 10. The final request spends 40 milliseconds waiting and 10 executing. A benchmark that starts its timer only when execution begins would omit most of that person's experience.",
        "This worker can finish at most 100 requests per second under these assumptions. If 120 arrive every second for a sustained period, unfinished work grows by roughly 20 per second once it is busy. No choice of order can make all that work fit. The system must add capacity, reduce work, slow incoming traffic, or refuse some requests. Refusing work can keep accepted requests responsive, but the refusal rate must remain visible in the results. Otherwise a system can appear fast by discarding difficult work.",
        "Matching capacity exactly can work in a perfectly regular schedule. If a request arrives every 10 milliseconds and each takes exactly 10 milliseconds, the worker finishes just as the next request arrives. None waits. But suppose the first request takes 15 milliseconds and all later ones take 10. The second arrives at time 10 and waits until 15; the third arrives at 20 and waits until 25. Every later request also waits five milliseconds. The queue does not keep growing in this example, but there is no spare time to remove the delay. Another disturbance could add more waiting.",
        "Arriving more slowly leaves room to recover. With arrivals every 12 milliseconds and the same one-time 15-millisecond first job, the first three requests wait 0, 3, and 1 milliseconds; the fourth does not wait. The two spare milliseconds between ordinary jobs let the worker catch up. These regular schedules are teaching cases, not guarantees for variable arrivals. Five requests arriving together can cause a queue even if the next second is quiet. Average requests per second alone does not describe the bursts, job sizes, or ordering needed to explain delays. Nor does one utilization percentage determine waiting time without a model of those variations.",
        "Now suppose each execution has a shared 6-millisecond setup cost plus 2 milliseconds for each request included. Running one request costs 8 milliseconds. Running four together costs 14 milliseconds. Grouping requests this way is batching. For a continuously available backlog, single-request execution finishes 125 requests per second; four-request batches finish about 286 per second. This is a rate comparison under the stated cost model, not a promise that every machine gets faster with larger batches.",
        "Consider four requests arriving at times 0, 3, 6, and 9 milliseconds. Executing them individually as soon as the worker is free produces completions at 8, 16, 24, and 32. Their arrival-to-completion delays are 8, 13, 18, and 23 milliseconds, averaging 15.5. Waiting until time 9 to assemble one batch produces a common completion time of 23. Their delays are then 23, 20, 17, and 14, averaging 18.5. The batch finishes the whole group sooner and occupies the worker for less time, but increases the average delay and makes the first request much slower.",
        "A batching policy therefore needs both a size limit and a waiting rule. Waiting forever to fill a large batch leaves the last few requests stuck when arrivals stop. A timer can launch a partly filled batch, trading some sharing benefit for bounded assembly time. That timer does not bound total delay: earlier batches can still occupy the device, and execution itself can vary. If users have deadlines, the policy must account for time already spent waiting and the work still ahead.",
        "Order also changes who benefits. Let one 100-millisecond job and ten 1-millisecond jobs all arrive together. With the long job first, the eleven completion delays sum to 1,155 milliseconds, for an average of 105. With the short jobs first, their delays sum to 55 and the long job finishes at 110, giving an overall average of 15. Both orders finish all work at time 110. Serving short jobs first improves this average dramatically, but a continuing stream of short jobs could keep a long job waiting indefinitely. Preventing that requires an additional rule, such as increasing priority with waiting time or reserving a share of service.",
        "Averages can hide the people who wait longest. If 99 requests finish in 10 milliseconds and one in 1,010 milliseconds, the mean is 20 milliseconds. That number describes neither the usual request nor the worst one. Report the distribution or clearly defined percentiles as well. A percentile states a delay below which a chosen fraction of observations falls; exact conventions differ for finite samples. Also report how many requests were rejected and whether the timer began at arrival, admission, or execution.",
        "For generated text, one request consists of repeated steps rather than one indivisible calculation. This creates opportunities to reconsider the group between steps. The OSDI 2022 Orca abstract describes scheduling at the level of an iteration and batching selected operations. Its motivation is that a fixed group can hold back finished requests and delay newly arriving ones. This is a historical mechanism example, not a performance prediction for today's services or for the numbers above. The practical limit is that changing groups still requires enough memory for the active requests and a schedule their operations can follow.",
        "When reading the MLSys serving themes, distinguish arrival-to-first-output delay, gaps between outputs, and time until the entire answer finishes. They answer different questions. A service that begins quickly but pauses repeatedly may have a good first-output number and a poor reading experience. Ask which requests share execution, how newcomers enter, what happens to long-running work, and what happens when demand exceeds capacity. Those policy choices belong in an end-to-end explanation alongside the kernel speed measurements.",
    ],
    "applications": [
        "Model serving: choose batch size and admission rules while accounting for memory and time already spent waiting.",
        "Shared clusters: compare scheduling policies using both completion delays and treatment of long jobs.",
        "Performance evaluation: include rejected work and state exactly where each timer starts and stops.",
    ],
    "exercise": {
        "question": "Three requests arrive at times 0, 2, and 4 milliseconds. Execution costs 4 milliseconds of setup plus 2 per request. Compare immediate individual execution with waiting until time 4 and executing one batch. Calculate completion times, individual delays, average delay, and total worker busy time. Which result would you report to someone who cares about the first request?",
        "answer": "Individual runs each take 6 milliseconds and finish at 6, 12, and 18. Subtracting arrivals gives delays of 6, 10, and 14, averaging 10. The worker is busy for 18 milliseconds. One three-request batch takes 10 milliseconds, starts at 4, and finishes at 14. Delays are 14, 12, and 10, averaging 12; busy time is 10. Batching finishes the group four milliseconds earlier and saves eight milliseconds of worker time, but the first request waits 14 rather than 6 milliseconds. Report that delay directly rather than substituting the group completion time or average rate.",
    },
    "sources": [
        ("https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi22/presentation/yu", "Yu and colleagues, Orca (OSDI 2022), abstract — iteration-level scheduling and selective batching"),
        ("analysis/mlsys-2025-first-principles-subthemes.md", "MLSys 2025 subthemes — serving schedules, request state, and resource allocation"),
    ],
})

SECTIONS.append({
    "id": "durable-update",
    "kicker": "Worked update",
    "title": "Follow an Update Through a Crash and a Retry",
    "summary": "Prerequisites: correctness and multi-machine recovery. Define what a success reply means, then check every interruption point.",
    "body": [
        "Consider a small inventory service with two locations. It holds 10 items at A and 5 at B. Request R asks to move three items from A to B. The required new state is A = 7 and B = 8. The total must remain 15, readers must not observe half a move, and once success is acknowledged the move must survive the specified failure. This is a teaching model, not production database code or a claim about a conference system.",
        "Name the failure first. Assume a single server can lose power between persistence steps, losing all ordinary memory. Previously confirmed durable storage survives. There is only one writer, readers are paused during an update and during recovery, and there are no malicious writes or later media corruption. Real services may need to handle concurrent writers, disk loss, and replicas; the procedure here establishes nothing about those additional cases.",
        "The tempting implementation writes A = 7 and then B = 8. If only the first write survives, the total becomes 12. Reversing the order permits a total of 18 instead. Keeping readers paused during normal execution does not fix the stored state after a crash. The missing property is an all-or-nothing transition that recovery can interpret, often called atomicity.",
        "Use fresh storage rather than overwriting the only good copy. Keep an old snapshot with A = 10 and B = 5. Prepare a new snapshot containing A = 7, B = 8, request identifier R, its exact operation, and its successful result. Keep completion records for earlier requests too. The new snapshot is a candidate, not yet the published state. Readers must continue using the old snapshot until publication.",
        "Now make two explicit storage assumptions. First, a persistence operation can confirm that every byte of the new snapshot has reached storage that survives the modeled power loss. Second, publishing a reference to that snapshot is itself an atomic durable operation: after a crash, recovery sees either the old reference or the new reference, never a damaged mixture. These are strong assumptions, not properties to infer from an ordinary assignment or file write. A real design must show how its storage interface supplies them.",
        "The server first persists the complete candidate and waits for confirmation. Only then does it publish the new reference and wait for that operation's durable completion. It then permits readers and sends success. A crash before publication leaves the old reference authoritative; an unused candidate can be ignored. A crash after publication leaves the new reference authoritative, and the complete candidate is available because persistence preceded publication. If the publication was interrupted, its atomicity assumption limits recovery to those same two cases.",
        "Durability and atomicity answer different questions. Atomicity says recovery does not expose a half-move. Durability says a move already acknowledged as successful remains present after the modeled failure. Before an acknowledgement arrives, either old or new state can be possible. After a valid acknowledgement, only the new state is permitted. Publishing and replying before persistence finishes could preserve normal reads but violate that durability promise after power loss.",
        "Now suppose publication succeeds, but the reply is lost. The caller cannot distinguish this from a request that never completed, so it retries using the same identifier R. If the service blindly executes the move again, A becomes 4 and B becomes 11. The total is still 15, yet the requested operation happened twice. Checking only the total misses the error. Recovery must retain enough information to distinguish a repeated request from a new move.",
        "Because the successful result for R is stored in the same published snapshot as the changed inventory, a retry finds that completion record and returns the recorded result without moving anything again. The identifier must be unique within a stated client or service scope, and the service must reject reuse of R for a different operation. This combines request identity and state change in one recovery decision. Storing them separately without an ordering or atomicity rule would reopen the same partial-update problem.",
        "Keeping completion records has a cost. Removing R too early allows a delayed retry to look new. A real service needs a retention rule, such as an agreed retry lifetime or a protocol confirming which requests a client will never repeat. Our small model retains all records and does not claim unbounded practical scalability. Similarly, old snapshots cannot be reclaimed while a reader or the recovery procedure still needs them.",
        "For a separate timing example, assume preparation costs 1 ms, candidate persistence 4 ms, durable publication 2 ms, and reply delivery 1 ms, all sequential. Success arrives after 8 ms. Improving preparation to 0.5 ms changes this to 7.5 ms, not 4 ms. Replying after preparation alone would appear faster by changing the promise. Grouping several updates into one persistence operation can share cost, but adds waiting and requires a defined order and completion record for every grouped request.",
        "OSTEP's crash-consistency chapter provides the foundational connection: related persistent structures can disagree after an interrupted update, and a structural repair need not restore the intended contents. Our snapshot publication and retry example is a separate simplified derivation with its own explicit assumptions. In the conference material, F2FSJ and WOFS address durable-state organization, while checking and replay papers address different parts of the evidence. These should not be treated as interchangeable recovery mechanisms.",
        "To evaluate a real proposal, interrupt execution at each persistence boundary and inspect the recovered state before serving new work. Repeat interruptions during recovery itself. Test lost replies and duplicate requests as well as power loss. Record the storage guarantees required, the normal execution cost, the restart cost, and any faults outside the model. Passing these selected tests is evidence for those cases, not a proof of all concurrent histories or all possible storage failures.",
    ],
    "table": {
        "caption": "Crash cases under the teaching model's atomic-publication and persistence assumptions",
        "headings": ["Interruption", "Authoritative snapshot", "Retry of R"],
        "rows": [
            ["Before candidate is durable", "Old: A 10, B 5", "Execute the move"],
            ["Candidate durable, not published", "Old: A 10, B 5", "Execute the move"],
            ["During atomic publication", "Old or new, never half", "Inspect completion record"],
            ["Published, reply missing", "New: A 7, B 8; R recorded", "Return recorded result"],
            ["Success acknowledged", "New: A 7, B 8; R recorded", "Return recorded result"],
        ],
    },
    "exercise": {
        "question": "A proposed variant publishes the new inventory first and writes the completion record for R afterward. Another variant persists R's completion record first and updates inventory afterward. Give a crash point that breaks each variant. Why is a total-items check insufficient?",
        "answer": "In the first variant, crash after inventory changes but before R is recorded. A retry can move the three items again because R looks new. In the second, crash after R is recorded but before inventory changes. A retry returns success even though the move never happened. Both can preserve the total of 15 while violating the operation's meaning. The inventory and completion record need one coordinated recovery rule; this lesson's single published snapshot supplies one only under its stated storage and serialization assumptions.",
    },
    "sources": [
        ("https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-journaling.pdf", "OSTEP, chapter 42, sections 42.1–42.2 — partial persistence and limits of structural repair"),
        ("analysis/osdi-asplos-2025-first-principles-service-traces.md", "Cross-conference durable-state trace — related mechanisms, not a tested combined stack"),
        ("analysis/osdi-2025-first-principles-subthemes.md", "OSDI subthemes — crash consistency, protocols, debugging, and replay"),
    ],
    "applications": [
        "Storage services: distinguish a returned write call from an acknowledged durable state change.",
        "Request APIs: preserve identity and completion information across retries and crashes.",
        "Paper evaluation: check interrupted operations and recovery costs alongside normal write throughput.",
    ],
})

SECTIONS.insert(next(i for i, section in enumerate(SECTIONS) if section['id'] == 's8'), {
    "id": "physical-design",
    "kicker": "Physical limits",
    "title": "A Calculation Needs Wires, Power, and Time to Settle",
    "summary": "Prerequisites: timing, memory, and topology. Connect an abstract operation to a physical implementation without confusing a model with a manufactured chip.",
    "body": [
        "Imagine adding two stored numbers. In a chip, their zeros and ones are represented by electrical signals. Small circuits combine those signals, wires carry the intermediate results, and storage circuits capture the answer. Each part takes up space, needs energy, and takes time to respond. To understand whether a proposed chip can perform the addition at the promised rate, follow that physical path.",
        "Separate power from energy first. Power is the rate of energy use, measured in watts; one watt is one joule per second. Energy is the accumulated use over time. In a teaching example, a device using 100 watts for two seconds consumes 200 joules. A faster device using 150 watts for one second consumes 150 joules. The faster device has higher power but lower energy for that task. Which one is acceptable also depends on the available electrical and cooling capacity.",
        "Keep the measurement boundary fixed. If the faster device additionally requires a host and memory consuming 80 watts during its one-second run, the included total becomes 230 joules. That does not prove it loses to the first device unless the first device's supporting components are included on the same basis. Idle time, startup, and cooling can matter as well. A chip-only energy number and a complete-system energy number answer different questions.",
        "Changing a signal's voltage requires moving charge onto or off the connected wires and circuit inputs. Capacitance measures how much charge a structure stores for a given voltage difference. In a simplified circuit model, the energy used to charge and discharge it scales with capacitance and voltage squared. Holding the number of these changes and the capacitance fixed, reducing voltage from 1.0 to 0.8 volts reduces this energy to 0.8² = 0.64 of its former value. That is not a 36% whole-system energy guarantee. Leakage, the current that flows even when a circuit is not switching, and other components also consume energy. MIT's linked circuit lesson derives the charging model.",
        "Lowering voltage can also make circuits respond more slowly. If the answer now arrives too late to be stored correctly, the machine needs more time per step. That longer run keeps supporting components powered for longer. Consider an invented example: switching uses 100 joules and supporting components use 20 watts during a one-second task. Total energy is 120 joules. Suppose lower voltage reduces switching energy to 64 joules but stretches the task to three seconds, with the same supporting power. The new total is 64 + 20 × 3 = 124 joules. Less energy per switch can coexist with more energy per completed task. These assumed durations illustrate the accounting; the voltage ratio alone does not predict them.",
        "Heat also has a history. A short run may finish before the device reaches its sustained temperature. A longer run can require reducing its operating rate to remain within a temperature limit. Suppose, as a teaching model, a device processes 100 items per second for ten seconds, then 60 per second for the next fifty. It completes 4,000 items in a minute, averaging about 66.7 per second. A design sustaining 80 per second completes 4,800. The first device's peak rate ranks it first; sustained completed work ranks it second.",
        "The pattern of activity matters along with the total. Concentrating switching in a small area can create a hot region even when average chip power looks acceptable. Many units switching together can also stress the network delivering power. A supply voltage specified at the board is not automatically the voltage available at every cell at every instant. These effects are reasons to examine placement, workload activity, and the power network rather than assuming a single nominal number captures all operation.",
        "Many chips use a repeating electrical signal, the clock, to tell storage circuits when to capture their inputs. The time between successive capture events is a clock cycle. A result must reach its destination and remain stable for a specified interval before capture; this is the setup requirement. It must also remain stable for a specified interval after capture; this is the hold requirement. MIT's sequential-logic lesson explains both. A longer cycle can give a late result more time to arrive, but does not by itself stop the next result from arriving too soon after capture. Timing checks must examine both the latest and earliest possible changes.",
        "A nanosecond is one billionth of a second. In a simplified teaching path, allow 0.6 nanoseconds for calculation, 0.3 for the wires, and 0.2 for storage-circuit delay and timing allowances. Its required interval is 1.1 nanoseconds, so it does not fit a 1-nanosecond cycle. Halving only the calculation delay changes the requirement to 0.8 nanoseconds, not 0.55. This example combines several contributions into one allowance; a real analysis separately handles clock arrival, setup and hold constraints, variation, and the actual paths. Passing this calculation establishes only that this simplified late-arrival limit is met.",
        "Now check the earliest change. In a separate teaching model, both storage circuits receive the clock event at time zero. The destination needs its old input to remain unchanged until 0.08 ns afterward, but the new input can arrive at 0.03 ns. That is 0.05 ns too early. Moving the next clock event from time 1 ns to time 2 ns leaves this conflict unchanged: the problem occurs just after the current event. A slower clock alone does not satisfy this hold requirement.",
        "Suppose an added delay element contributes exactly 0.06 ns in this idealized model. The earliest change moves to 0.09 ns and satisfies the 0.08-ns hold requirement. But delayed signals also arrive later. If the path's latest-arrival requirement was 0.8 ns, it becomes 0.86 ns, still within a 1-ns cycle. Both checks pass here. Real delay elements have ranges that depend on conditions, so a repair must be checked for both its earliest and latest effects, including differences in when the clock reaches each storage circuit.",
        "Wires are not free edges in a diagram. They occupy routing space and add resistance and capacitance, which influence delay and energy. Moving connected blocks closer can shorten a path, but crowding blocks can make their wires detour or leave too little space for the power network. Adding more arithmetic units can therefore increase congestion without increasing useful completed work. Placement chooses locations; routing chooses the physical connections between them.",
        "OpenROAD is software for turning a circuit description into a physical chip layout. Its documented sequence starts by setting the chip area and reserving space for major blocks and power connections: floorplanning. Placement assigns circuit locations. Clock distribution builds the connections that carry the clock to storage circuits. Routing chooses wire paths. Once those paths exist, the tools estimate their electrical effects and repeat timing and physical checks. A wire that takes a longer route than expected can change an earlier timing estimate. The documentation is a source for the flow, not evidence that any design in this course has completed it.",
        "Different checks establish different things. Functional simulation runs a model on chosen inputs. A timing analysis asks whether modeled signal paths meet stated timing constraints. A design-rule check examines geometry against supplied manufacturing rules. A layout-versus-schematic check compares extracted connectivity with the intended circuit. Passing one does not imply passing the others: correctly connected circuitry can still be too slow, and legal geometry can implement the wrong logic. All depend on the models, constraints, rules, and exclusions used.",
        "A post-layout simulation includes electrical effects derived from a particular layout; it is still a simulation. A chip measurement observes manufactured hardware, but only the sampled devices, workloads, and conditions were measured. Manufacturing variation means one successful chip does not establish the fraction of all manufactured chips that will meet the target. Report the evidence stage, tested conditions, and missing coverage rather than using the word validated without qualification.",
        "For the DAC, DATE, VLSID, and circuit-oriented conference material, follow this chain: intended behavior, circuit representation, physical arrangement, predicted behavior, and measured behavior where available. Ask where the reported gain was established and which later stages might change it. A placement improvement can be valuable before fabrication, but its evidence should remain a placement result. A measured energy saving should preserve task quality, operating duration, and system boundary in its comparison.",
    ],
    "exercise": {
        "question": "Design A completes a fixed task in 4 seconds using 60 watts at the measured device. Design B takes 3 seconds at 75 watts. First compare device energy. Then include a common host consuming 20 watts throughout each run. Finally, suppose B needs 1 additional second of preparation at 50 watts total system power, while A has no additional preparation. Which has lower end-to-end energy, and which finishes sooner?",
        "answer": "Device energy is 240 J for A and 225 J for B. Including the host gives (60 + 20) × 4 = 320 J for A and (75 + 20) × 3 = 285 J for B. B's additional preparation adds 50 J, taking its total to 335 J and its elapsed time to four seconds. A now uses less energy and they tie in completion time. This assumes equal accepted results and includes only the stated intervals and components; changing those boundaries changes the question.",
    },
    "sources": [
        ("https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c8/c8s1/", "MIT Computation Structures, Design Tradeoffs — charging energy and switching activity; lesson quantities are teaching examples"),
        ("https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c5/c5s1/", "MIT Computation Structures, Sequential Logic — capture, setup, hold, and clock timing"),
        ("https://openroad.readthedocs.io/en/latest/main/README.html", "OpenROAD documentation — physical implementation stages and final checks"),
        ("analysis/dac-2025-first-principles-synthesis.md", "DAC 2025 synthesis — representation, physical execution, and source-coverage limits"),
        ("analysis/date-2025-first-principles-synthesis.md", "DATE 2025 synthesis — physical constraints and validation boundaries"),
    ],
    "applications": [
        "Device comparisons: distinguish peak power, task energy, and sustained throughput.",
        "Architecture proposals: include data movement, layout, and power delivery alongside arithmetic units.",
        "Research reading: identify whether a result comes from a model, a routed design, or measured hardware.",
    ],
})

SECTIONS.append({
    "id": "training-workflow",
    "kicker": "Worked training",
    "title": "Follow a Training Update to an Accepted Model",
    "summary": "Prerequisites: dependencies, numerical precision, coordination, and recovery. Separate the time for one update from the time required to reach the target quality.",
    "body": [
        "Training repeatedly changes a model using examples. In this simplified synchronous procedure, four workers start each step from the same model version, process separate portions of a batch, combine their update contributions, and apply one agreed update. The next step uses the resulting version. This is one teaching procedure, not a claim that every training algorithm must follow it. All timings below are invented.",
        "Define the final result before timing the loop. Suppose the job must reach a specified quality threshold on held-out evaluation data, using a stated evaluation rule. The threshold, dataset, and rule remain fixed across the compared methods. Completed steps are intermediate work; the accepted model is the result. Faster steps do not necessarily reduce time to that result if the algorithm needs more of them.",
        "For one baseline step, input preparation takes 8 ms. The workers then calculate in parallel for 24, 24, 24, and 40 ms. After all contributions are ready, exchange and combination take 12 ms; applying and checking the update takes 4 ms. Assume these stages are sequential except for the workers' parallel calculation, no other contention, and no hidden setup. Step time is 8 + max(24, 24, 24, 40) + 12 + 4 = 64 ms.",
        "The first three workers each wait 16 ms for the fourth. Their mean calculation time is 28 ms, but using 28 in the step equation would predict 52 ms, which is not a possible completion time under this schedule. The next operation requires every contribution. This is why a mean device-utilization measure can miss the worker that determines progress.",
        "Imagine redistributing work so all four calculations take 28 ms, with unchanged inputs and accepted update semantics. If moving the work adds a 5-ms non-overlapped preparation cost, the step becomes 8 + 5 + 28 + 12 + 4 = 57 ms. The ideal calculation saving was 12 ms; seven remain after movement. If redistribution instead costs 15 ms, total time becomes 67 ms and the change loses. A balanced assignment is not automatically a faster assignment.",
        "What does preserving the update require? In a separate numerical example, three workers each process one training example and report an average contribution of 2. The fourth processes nine examples and reports an average contribution of 10. Suppose all contributions were calculated from the same model version, every example should have equal weight, and the required combined value is their mean. Averaging the four worker reports gives (2 + 2 + 2 + 10)/4 = 4. But the mean over the twelve examples is (1 × 2 + 1 × 2 + 1 × 2 + 9 × 10)/12 = 8. The first calculation gave a one-example worker as much influence as the nine-example worker. Changing the assignment therefore requires checking how contributions are combined, not just where they are calculated. These numbers are invented; another training rule may deliberately use different weights.",
        "Now examine a separate overlap proposal on the original assignment. Suppose the first exchange chunk becomes ready at time 28 ms, twenty milliseconds into the worker-calculation interval. It transfers from 28 to 34 while independent calculation continues. A second chunk is available only when all workers finish at time 48 and transfers from 48 to 54. The final update then runs from 54 to 58. Six milliseconds of transfer are hidden; the other six remain on the completion path. This requires the stated chunk readiness, sufficient buffers, and resources that can transfer without slowing calculation.",
        "Taking max(40, 12) for the entire calculation-and-exchange interval would give 8 + max(40, 12) + 4 = 52 ms for the step, but that optimistic bound is not achieved by this schedule: the final transfer cannot start before time 48, so the step actually takes 58 ms. Calling an exchange asynchronous does not make its data available earlier. Neither may we automatically combine the 57-ms balanced schedule with this overlap plan: redistribution changes when chunks are ready, so the combined dependency timeline must be constructed again.",
        "A representation change adds a different trade. Starting from the baseline with no overlap, suppose compression cuts exchange from 12 to 6 ms but encoding and decoding add 3 ms. The resulting step takes 8 + 40 + 3 + 6 + 4 = 61 ms. If the baseline reaches target quality in 1,000 steps, it uses 64 seconds of step execution. If compression requires 1,200 steps to reach the same target, it uses 73.2 seconds. The step is faster but the accepted model arrives later. Actual step counts must be measured, not assumed from this example.",
        "Holding those illustrative step times constant, compression wins on step-execution time only when its required count is below 64,000/61, about 1,049.18 steps. Thus 1,049 steps finish slightly sooner and 1,050 finish later than the 1,000-step baseline. Real comparisons also include startup, periodic evaluation, checkpoints, and any failure recovery. These may occur at different frequencies after the procedure changes.",
        "Memory can change the feasible schedule. Input prefetching needs storage for a future batch; overlapping exchange needs buffers for data already produced; training also holds model parameters and other update state. A policy that uses all free memory for larger batches may remove the space an overlap plan assumed. Define which values must coexist, not just the largest individual array. Changing batch size can also change the sequence of updates and the number needed to reach target quality.",
        "A stopped worker raises a state question before a speed question. If some workers applied the new update while another still holds the old version, restarting them without reconciliation does not resume the assumed synchronous procedure. A saved restart point needs the model version and all procedure-specific state required to continue, such as update history, random-generator state, and input position when those affect the promised continuation. The exact requirement depends on whether recovery promises identical replay or only an acceptable resumed training run.",
        "The local conference trace links different research questions: WLB-LLM discusses assignment, FSMoE discusses coordinated expert work, ZEN discusses communicated updates under a quality condition, and communication papers examine paths or scheduling. Those are leads to separate mechanisms. This tutorial's schedules are not measurements of those systems and do not establish that the systems compose. A combined proposal needs a shared implementation, workload, quality rule, and failure policy.",
        "A complete final experiment reports time to the accepted quality target, the quality trajectory, resource use, and failed or incomplete runs under stated stopping rules. Include randomness and repeated trials where they affect the conclusion. Then use the step timeline to explain the observed difference. A faster isolated operation helps explain a result, but does not replace the result itself.",
    ],
    "table": {
        "caption": "Baseline teaching timeline for one synchronous update",
        "headings": ["Event", "Time from step start", "Condition for continuing"],
        "rows": [
            ["Prepare inputs", "0–8 ms", "All assigned inputs ready"],
            ["Worker calculation", "8–48 ms", "Every contribution ready; three workers finish at 32 ms"],
            ["Exchange and combine", "48–60 ms", "Combined contribution available"],
            ["Apply and check update", "60–64 ms", "Required workers have accepted the next version"],
        ],
    },
    "exercise": {
        "question": "Starting from the 64-ms baseline step, halve only the slowest worker's calculation from 40 to 20 ms. The other workers still take 24 ms. What is the new step time? If this modified procedure takes 1,300 steps to reach the same quality target while the baseline takes 1,000, which has lower step-execution time?",
        "answer": "The new maximum worker time is 24 ms, not 20. The step takes 8 + 24 + 12 + 4 = 48 ms. At 1,300 steps it consumes 62,400 ms, or 62.4 seconds, versus the baseline's 64 seconds. It wins by 1.6 seconds on the stated boundary, not by the slowest worker's twofold calculation speedup. Additional preparation, evaluation, or recovery differences could reverse that narrow result and must be counted if the claim concerns the entire job.",
    },
    "sources": [("analysis/osdi-asplos-2025-first-principles-service-traces.md", "Distributed-training service trace — separate paper mechanisms and explicit composition limits")],
    "applications": [
        "Distributed training: explain a step through its slowest dependencies and required exchanges.",
        "Algorithm/system comparisons: measure time to the same quality target, not only time per step.",
        "Recovery design: state whether saved information supports identical replay or another permitted continuation.",
    ],
})

